language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ three_coins: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Three coins lie on a table in the order tails, heads, tails. In precisely three moves make them face either all heads or all tails. Each move consists of choosing one coin and flipping it over.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `three_coins.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/three_coins.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `three_coins_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py three_coins.json
$   savilerow model.eprime three_coins_1.param -run-solver

$ number of moves to make
given num_moves : int(1..)
$ number of configurations to report: the start and one after each move
letting num_configs = num_moves + 1
$ init[i]: the starting face of coin i, 1-based (the data's index 0 is init[1]); 1 is tails, 0 is heads
given init : matrix indexed by [int(1..3)] of int(0..1)

$ steps[t, i]: coin i after t moves (t = 1 is the start, the data's index 0), true when it shows tails, false for heads
find steps : matrix indexed by [int(1..num_configs), int(1..3)] of bool

such that
$ the start configuration is the initial one: each coin shows the face it starts with
forAll i : int(1..3) . steps[1, i] <-> (init[i] = 1),
$ each move flips exactly one coin
forAll t : int(1..num_moves) . (sum i : int(1..3) . toInt(steps[t, i] != steps[t + 1, i])) = 1,
$ after the last move all three coins face the same way, all heads or all tails
steps[num_configs, 1] = steps[num_configs, 2] /\ steps[num_configs, 2] = steps[num_configs, 3]
